@page "/remote-support"
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Remote support and remote access software";
    ViewData["Description"] = "Remote access software under your brand: your customers' Windows computers, attended or unattended, every monitor, files both ways, admin prompts.";
    ViewData["Keywords"] = "remote support software, remote access software, remote access to computer, remote desktop, unattended remote access, AnyDesk alternative, TeamViewer alternative, South Africa";
}
<section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Remote support and remote access</h1>
        <p class="lede">Your customer presses <strong>Request Support</strong> in an app with your name on it; every technician who is signed in
            hears it; whoever answers is on their screen in seconds. Or reach their computers when nobody is there — unattended remote
            access, only if they have allowed it. Your IT customers, the people who use your software, your own office and branches:
            the same app, under your brand.</p>
        <div class="next-links"><partial name="_SiteCta" /></div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" style="padding-bottom:0">
    <div class="wrap">
        <figure class="shot-figure shot-wide">
            <partial name="_Shot" model="SitePages.Shots.Session" />
            <figcaption>A session: the customer's screen in your console. The bar at the top of their screen says who is connected.</figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Attended support: they ask, you answer</h2>
        <div class="facts" style="margin-top:18px">
            <div class="card fact"><h3>A call that rings everybody</h3><p>A support request says what is wrong, with an optional picture of their screen, and rings every technician signed in until one takes it. Nobody waits in a queue.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>They allow it, every time</h3><p>A prompt in your colours asks the customer to allow the connection, and says who is connecting. Nothing is seen until they say yes.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Chat before, during and after</h3><p>Customers can chat with your team from the tray app without a session, with a ding for each message.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>More than one technician</h3><p>Two technicians can be in the same session — for a hand-over, a second opinion, or training.</p></div>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot-figure" style="margin-top:34px">
            <div class="shot-row">
                <partial name="_Shot" model="SitePages.Shots.CustomerApp" />
                <partial name="_Shot" model="SitePages.Shots.Request" />
                <partial name="_Shot" model="SitePages.Shots.Allow" />
            </div>
            <figcaption>On the customer's computer: your app, the request with a picture of their screen, and the prompt that asks them to allow it.</figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Unattended access: their switch, their password</h2>
        <p class="lede">A background service on the computer lets you connect when nobody is at it — at the lock screen, after hours, on a server.
            It does nothing until the customer ticks <em>allow unattended access</em> in their own app, and they can untick it any time.</p>
        <div class="facts" style="margin-top:18px">
            <div class="card fact"><h3>A password of their own</h3><p>A customer can set an unattended password that every technician must type. A technician who types it right once is not asked again — a colleague still is.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Look without connecting</h3><p>Right-click a computer to see its running programs and its performance — processor, memory, disks and network — without opening a session.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Wake it up</h3><p>An offline computer can be woken by another of the same customer's computers on the same network.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Exit means offline</h3><p>When the customer exits your app, the computer goes offline for everybody until they open it again.</p></div>
        </div>
        <figure class="shot-figure" style="margin-top:34px">
            <div class="shot-mid"><partial name="_Shot" model="SitePages.Shots.Performance" /></div>
            <figcaption>Look without connecting: a computer's processor, memory, disk and network, live.</figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>In the session</h2>
        <ul class="feature-list" style="margin-top:18px">
            <li>Every monitor, sharp and fast (H.264), and their real mouse pointer</li>
            <li>Copy and paste text and files, both ways</li>
            <li>Drag files on and off their screen</li>
            <li>A file browser for their computer</li>
            <li>Print on your printer from their computer — and on theirs from yours</li>
            <li>Talk to them, and hear their computer's sound</li>
            <li>Ctrl+Alt+Del, the lock screen and admin prompts</li>
            <li>Windows shortcuts and Alt+Tab go to their computer</li>
            <li>Restart, and the session comes back by itself</li>
            <li>Scripts (PowerShell), with the output as it runs</li>
            <li>A privacy screen: their monitor goes black while you work</li>
            <li>Record the session, and keep it on the ticket</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap grid2">
        <div>
            <h2>Help from outside, on their terms</h2>
            <p>Sometimes a customer's accountant or software vendor needs to look. The customer makes a code in your app and gives it to them;
                they connect from their own copy, or from a browser — screen and control only, files only if the customer ticks it, and labelled
                as an outsider everywhere.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h2>No app installed yet?</h2>
            <p>A customer can share their screen from a browser on your <a href="/website-chat">support page</a> — view only, with talking — and
                you can take it from there.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Your brand, all the way through</h2>
        <p>Your logo, colours, product name and welcome message on the customer's app, the prompts, the download page, the installer and your
            technicians' console. Change them in the portal and every installed computer picks them up — nobody reinstalls anything.</p>
        <div class="next-links"><a class="btn" href="/ticketing">Next: ticketing and the workshop</a> <a class="btn" href="/security">How it is kept secure</a></div>
    </div>
</section>
